      "id": 1,
      "text": "Emmetsburg takes its name from Irish nationalist Robert Emmet, executed at 25 for leading the 1803 rebellion against Britain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emmetsburg%2C_Iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A bronze statue of Robert Emmet watches over the Palo Alto County Courthouse Square, a rare piece of international history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.traveliowa.com/emmetsburgs-charm-and-must-dos/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Emmetsburg was first settled by Irish immigrants fleeing the Great Famine between 1845 and 1852.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emmetsburg%2C_Iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the winter of 1855‑56, a Sioux band led by Chief Old Sleepy‑Eye camped just 50 rods from the Des Moines River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://emmetsburg.com/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Martin Coonan erected the first tavern and post office in Old Town, carving a hand‑pointing sign that read 'Emmetsburg'.",
